cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Flatshare, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: Flatshare
abstract: >-
  Flatshare measures a self-hosted voice agent (speech to text, an LLM, text to
  speech) sharing one GPU with long-context coding agents, and changes vLLM's
  priority scheduling to bring the voice tail back near its SLO. On vLLM 0.32,
  beside 24 coding-agent sessions on one RTX 5060 Ti 16 GB, the voice-to-voice
  p95 of two calls rises from 1.78 s alone to 10.5 s, because a waiting voice
  turn cannot take KV cache from running agent requests and agent prefill
  shares every step of its reply. Two scheduler rules, proposed to vLLM as pull
  requests #63350 and #63351 (both open, with no maintainer review as of
  2026-10-29), let a waiting higher-priority request preempt lower-priority work
  for KV and keep lower-priority prefill out of the steps of its first tokens.
  They bring the p95 to 2.24 s, 0.20 s over an SLO of voice alone plus 250 ms,
  while the agents keep 97% of their throughput; on an A100 SXM4 80 GB with the
  same model, KV pool and agents, they bring it from 4.99 s to 1.63 s, under
  that card's SLO in each of five rounds. The repository holds the scheduler
  variant for vLLM 0.32.0, the gateway that is the serving control plane, the
  measurement harness, a KV cache simulator, the deployment, and the committed
  records from which its tables and figures are rebuilt.
authors:
